{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals","id":1163600,"title":"Vitae effects on mortals","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-29T10:18:22-08:00","id":1163600,"title":"Vitae effects on mortals","content":"So, a player wants to blood-bond an elderly couple without ghouling them, and wanted to know what effect it would have beyond addiction. Good question; a very quick perusal of the book didn't yield an answer. I wouldn't necessarily give it stat bonuses, but it should have some describable effect. A mild-to-moderate euphoriant, sure. Maybe a slight stasis effect, in terms of longevity and physical degeneration, but not outside the range of human norms. Odd dreams, and if the user is under the influence of any other mind-altering substance, the Curse skews the hallucinations. Of course, healing, I should think, but no disciplines. What would you do?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-29T10:37:49-08:00","id":1163605,"title":"","content":"Without ghouling them no more effects than the Blood Bond. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-29T10:44:03-08:00","id":1163609,"title":"","content":"Yes, having Vitae as healing potions doesn't seem like the way I'd go. I certainly describe it in enough detail to point out that 'it tastes good' and better than anything they would experience outside of another drop, but realistically the Persistent Addiction Condition should do that.<br /> <br /> There is a bonus Condition in Half-Damned for drinking Vitae.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 11-29-2017, 04:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T01:39:45-08:00","id":1163845,"title":"","content":"Machanicly they would gain Vinculum and Vitae Addiction, beyond that you have some very good ideas, remember that the blood can course as many problems as is solves so they could for example be compelled to travel to the vampires haven without knowing why or try and keep him from doing something they think of as dangerous even if they know it wouldn't be for him.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T01:55:17-08:00","id":1163848,"title":"","content":"You're confusing blood bond with ghouling. Though, you can do everything you want in your chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23530-dante-ru","name":"Dante[RU]","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T06:22:34-08:00","id":1163888,"title":"","content":"If you're not ghouling them, then they would not gain the benefits of healing (which takes the expenditure of an internal vitae point... which you got from your regent as a ghoul). They would also not have enough vitae in their system to retard aging (another ghoul thing) or manifest Vigor, etc. <br /> <br /> They would suffer the effects of the blood bond, which is likely to be negative to their mutual relationship as their thoughts and attention shift to the donor. That means living together will become more difficult, minor things blow up more, and decades of transgressions become &quot;un-forgiven&quot;. Not pretty to see such a rapid emotional breakdown.<br /> <br /> Whether there is vitae addiction that sets in would be partially determined by the addiction rolls on drinking. Since we're talking about small quantities (or at least, quantities not charged with the Willpower expenditure needed for ghouling), then the addiction roll could be at a bonus.<br /> <br /> What is certain is that they are now forever &quot;tainted&quot; by vitae, and when they die (of old age or complications), there is a chance that they will (individually or together) wake back up. Revenants.<br /> <br /> --Khanwuf","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T07:34:51-08:00","id":1163908,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals#post1163600\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, a player wants to blood-bond an elderly couple without ghouling them, and wanted to know what effect it would have beyond addiction. Good question; a very quick perusal of the book didn't yield an answer. I wouldn't necessarily give it stat bonuses, but it should have some describable effect. A mild-to-moderate euphoriant, sure. Maybe a slight stasis effect, in terms of longevity and physical degeneration, but not outside the range of human norms. Odd dreams, and if the user is under the influence of any other mind-altering substance, the Curse skews the hallucinations. Of course, healing, I should think, but no disciplines. What would you do?</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Vitae is life. Life tastes very, very good. In fact, it tastes so good, it\u2019s highly addictive to both mortal and Kindred alike.&quot; Pg 90<br /> &quot;Vitae addiction feels very similar to addiction to a strong drug.&quot; -- Now you might not interpret it the way I do, but if it looks like a duck, tastes like a duck, and quacks like a duck... it's probably a duck. In my games I have equated vitae to the drugs that it implies it is. Ecstasy, Heroine, Cocaine... except way way better tasting and feeling. I would say an extreme &quot;Euphoriant&quot;<br /> <br /> One can not say that it has zero effects other than &quot;blood bonding&quot; because if it was like drinking water... it wouldn't be addicting. it tastes good. It feels good. It gives you a fix and a feeling of temporary euphoria that you want to have again and again and again. That's how addictions work. Like Khan said they will grow irritable and easily annoyed with small things.<br /> <br /> <br /> However mechanically 1 vitae is still 1 vitae with or without the willpower spent to make it a ghoul. The rules in the book are very clear on how to proceed with the roll. Since the book doesn't deal with .25 vitae or .5 vitae, then there would be no roll for addiction if the amount was less than 1.<br /> <br /> RAW: No healing potions as vitae doesn't last long out of a vampire. It becomes inert. No anti aging, unless you are ghouling. No stat bonuses even if you did ghoul. No funky dreams, though with some drugs, you can dream strange things if you fall asleep high... so maybe on that one. Unless you ghouled this addict, there are no disciplines either.<br /> <br /> As Dante said, you might be confusing addiction with ghouling.<br /> <br /> <br /> When a mortal drinks vitae, one two or three things can happen and they are all very different things. <br /> Blood Bond comes in 3 stages -- automatically happens and a mortal can not stop it. 1 stage per night ( or scene if you'd like, the jury is out on that. )<br /> Vitae Addiction -- he must roll Resolve + Composure, with a \u20131 die penalty for each point consumed. - might not happen<br /> Ghoul -- this is a purposeful willful act and it can not happen on accident.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2308,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","user":"Vitalis","date":". 11-30-2017, 10:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T19:08:50-08:00","id":1164140,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1163908#post1163908\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As Dante said, you might be confusing addiction with ghouling.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not confusing anything, just experimenting with potential options that the books don't explore.<br /> <br /> I'm looking at the body of evidence. From a strictly mechanics standpoint, Vitae exposure at the time of death can cause a monumental supernatural change to the flesh and (depending on your cosmology) spirit. Prior Vitae exposure can occasionally cause undeath as well. Vitae provides a mystical, chemically undetectable change in the body that psychically ties a mortal to the undead provider of the Vitae, and with a effort of will that effect can be focused to give the mortal supernatural abilities powered by the Vitae.<br /> <br /> So, my take, based on both mechanics and available descriptions, and the fact that this is ostensibly a horror game, is that Vitae is a potent, insidious substance capable of significant alterations to living physiology is such a way that one might see it as a living entity itself. So, all potential additions to the effects of Vitae are simply extrapolations.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1163908#post1163908\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> &quot;Vitae addiction feels very similar to addiction to a strong drug.&quot; -- Now you might not interpret it the way I do, but if it looks like a duck, tastes like a duck, and quacks like a duck... it's probably a duck. In my games I have equated vitae to the drugs that it implies it is. Ecstasy, Heroine, Cocaine... except way way better tasting and feeling. I would say an extreme &quot;Euphoriant&quot;<br /> <br /> One can not say that it has zero effects other than &quot;blood bonding&quot; because if it was like drinking water... it wouldn't be addicting. it tastes good. It feels good. It gives you a fix and a feeling of temporary euphoria that you want to have again and again and again. That's how addictions work. Like Khan said they will grow irritable and easily annoyed with small things.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1163908#post1163908\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">RAW: No healing potions as vitae doesn't last long out of a vampire. It becomes inert.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well... If it because truly inert, without effect, then blood bonds wouldn't be a thing. So why couldn't it have a short term effect, such as healing on the spot but not later.<br /> <br /> Anyway, what I was thinking about re: healing (and I still haven't decided if it's something I'd want to spring on the players) with Vitae, if it works, will have some sort of supernatural side effect to it. Just off the top of my head: <i>you get a long knife rammed through your midsection. You're going to bleed out in a matter of minutes. Your secretive underworld contact appears in your tunneling vision and forces something cold and bitter, yet very compelling, down your throat. You live to get to the hospital, and you're back on your feet faster than you have any right to be. Funny thing, though. That pasty-yellowish spot on your abdomen, the scar from the attack, has no feeling whatsoever. More to the point, it is always ambient temperature-- on a cold day, a thermal image shows you glowing, with a black slit on your belly. stranger still, your liver has the same cool, insensate, nearly-necrotic patch on it.</i><br /> <br /> Apart from healed wounds, a connection to the vampire and possible addiction, there's no system involvement. But on a story level, can you imagine?<br /> &quot;What the hell did you do to me?&quot;<br /> &quot;I've never seen a scar like this. If I could run some tests, I could get a paper out of it.&quot;<br /> &quot;He's a walking masquerade breach; you must eliminate him before the Scourge see it.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1163609#post1163609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I certainly describe it in enough detail to point out that 'it tastes good' and better than anything they would experience outside of another drop, but realistically the Persistent Addiction Condition should do that.<br /> <br /> There is a bonus Condition in Half-Damned for drinking Vitae.</div> </div> </div> </div>Player: &quot;I try some meth.&quot;<br /> ST: &quot;You feel really good. Make your roll. You failed?&quot; &lt;slides Condition card across the table&gt; &quot;Congratulations, you're addicted.&quot;<br /> <br /> I guess I want a little more depth and creep factor than &quot;it tastes really good so you have to do what the scary man says.&quot; That's why I suggested things like funky dreams or other little tweaks that don't involve dice.<br /> <br /> I've gotten Half-Damned, but haven't had a chance to peruse. Looking forward to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2203,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","user":"Corrigan","date":". 11-30-2017, 10:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T19:21:08-08:00","id":1164146,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164140#post1164140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Player: &quot;I try some meth.&quot;<br /> ST: &quot;You feel really good. Make your roll. You failed?&quot; &lt;slides Condition card across the table&gt; &quot;Congratulations, you're addicted.&quot;<br /> <br /> I guess I want a little more depth and creep factor than &quot;it tastes really good so you have to do what the scary man says.&quot; That's why I suggested things like funky dreams or other little tweaks that don't involve dice.<br /> <br /> I've gotten Half-Damned, but haven't had a chance to peruse. Looking forward to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I put it in half quotes because I didn't want to type out a whole description which wouldn't make sense in an out of context setting to someone I know nothing about. It's more than 'it tastes good' it's the most fantastic thing you've ever experienced, but I don't know anything you've experienced beyond wanting more from a rule in a book.<br /> <br /> But it doesn't make you 'have to do what the scary man says' anyways. You're an addict, you're addicted to the vampire and their Vitae, you're not hypnotized.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 11-30-2017, 10:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T19:50:11-08:00","id":1164159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164146#post1164146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But it doesn't make you 'have to do what the scary man says' anyways. You're an addict, you're addicted to the vampire and their Vitae, you're not hypnotized.</div> </div> </div> </div>You do if you want another hit. Not to mention you're also bloodbound. :-)<br /> <br /> But it's true, I haven't been addicted to drugs, I only know people with addiction issues.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-30T20:00:55-08:00","id":1164160,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164159#post1164159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You do if you want another hit. Not to mention you're also bloodbound. :-)<br /> <br /> But it's true, I haven't been addicted to drugs, I only know people with addiction issues.</div> </div> </div> </div>Have you ever used a hardcore pain killer? Had an orgasm? Done a (powerful) drug even if you haven't been addicted? Asked out someone you had a deep crush/infatuation with and had them say yes? Come out on top in a heated competition? It's all of these things mixed in fluid form.<br /> <br /> What I meant by describing it as 'it tastes good' is not saying 'It tastes good.' I mean describing it in such a way that a player appreciates their character likes it, this Vitae is giving them something they want, that can make any down side of their life be pushed to the side by the simple taste of a drop of blood. It makes them want to suck on a walking corpse and drink their dark magic blood. But it helps if you know the character when you're describing this, and there's a situation.<br /> <br /> Your situation seems to be a player is seeing the effects of it in an elderly couple, so a description of its taste won't really matter but the way it impacts them can. They want it because it makes them feel full of life again, even if it doesn't actually make them young or anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-01T08:34:56-08:00","id":1164286,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164140#post1164140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not confusing anything, just experimenting with potential options that the books don't explore.<br /> <br /> I'm looking at the body of evidence. From a strictly mechanics standpoint, Vitae exposure at the time of death can cause a monumental supernatural change to the flesh and (depending on your cosmology) spirit. Prior Vitae exposure can occasionally cause undeath as well. Vitae provides a mystical, chemically undetectable change in the body that psychically ties a mortal to the undead provider of the Vitae, and with a effort of will that effect can be focused to give the mortal supernatural abilities powered by the Vitae.<br /> <br /> So, my take, based on both mechanics and available descriptions, and the fact that this is ostensibly a horror game, is that Vitae is a potent, insidious substance capable of significant alterations to living physiology is such a way that one might see it as a living entity itself. So, all potential additions to the effects of Vitae are simply extrapolations.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mechanically speaking Vitae can not heal anyone but the owner of it. A vampire needs to have it in his system to heal. A ghoul, the same. A human can not simply drink down a cup of vitae and heal. As per the mechanical rules as they are written. Now, should you want to do that in your game, you are more than welcome to. I change rules and I alter things to. I am sure every one does here and there. I just like to know what the rules are suppose to be and what I am changing them to. Especially if you have a rule monger in your game, you might need to give them a heads up on the matter. Do not take opinion as fact. Do not take story driven fluff as mechanical RAW.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164140#post1164140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Well... If it because truly inert, without effect, then blood bonds wouldn't be a thing. So why couldn't it have a short term effect, such as healing on the spot but not later.<br /> <br /> Anyway, what I was thinking about re: healing (and I still haven't decided if it's something I'd want to spring on the players) with Vitae, if it works, will have some sort of supernatural side effect to it. Just off the top of my head: <i>you get a long knife rammed through your midsection. You're going to bleed out in a matter of minutes. Your secretive underworld contact appears in your tunneling vision and forces something cold and bitter, yet very compelling, down your throat. You live to get to the hospital, and you're back on your feet faster than you have any right to be. Funny thing, though. That pasty-yellowish spot on your abdomen, the scar from the attack, has no feeling whatsoever. More to the point, it is always ambient temperature-- on a cold day, a thermal image shows you glowing, with a black slit on your belly. stranger still, your liver has the same cool, insensate, nearly-necrotic patch on it.</i><br /> <br /> Apart from healed wounds, a connection to the vampire and possible addiction, there's no system involvement. But on a story level, can you imagine?<br /> &quot;What the hell did you do to me?&quot;<br /> &quot;I've never seen a scar like this. If I could run some tests, I could get a paper out of it.&quot;<br /> &quot;He's a walking masquerade breach; you must eliminate him before the Scourge see it.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>If you want to look at this Mechanically:<br /> <br /> <b>&quot;Kindred Vitae</b> is a dangerous food. First off, Vitae is addicting<br /> (see Vitae Addiction, p. 99). As well, it causes the blood bond<br /> (see Blood Bonds, p. 90). However, it\u2019s efficient. Every Vitae<br /> taken is a Vitae earned, and this causes no additional damage<br /> to the victim. Stored Kindred Vitae is <i><u>effectively cold blood</u></i>. &quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Cold blood</b> from a corpse or kept outside the body for<br /> more than a few minutes (but no more than a night) is far<br /> less satisfying. To gain a single point of Vitae, a vampire must<br /> consume as many pints of this blood as her Blood Potency times<br /> two. <u><i>Technologically stored blood, for example in refrigerated<br /> plasma bags, is similarly unpalatable to potent Kindred.</i></u><br /> <br /> Whatever magic is in the blood... dies. As per RAW. It effectively becomes cold blood. Meaning it gains the effective qualities of it. Meaning it is no longer effective. Maybe the fact that the vitae is in a human it stays alive, but once it leaves it's life source it dies.. Even if a human has it one time, maybe that tiny little piece of it stays alive and that's enough to spawn a revenant, but that is not an &quot;Always&quot; event. it is a rare one.<br /> <br /> Blood bonds are not just about the blood. It's about the metaphysical magic that is carried within it. think of it like a parasite... it needs a host. A cup is not a host. A glass vial is not a host. A kindred, a werewolf, a human, are all viable hosts, probably because they have life in them... whatever that life may be.<br /> <br /> Raw mechanics say that you need to be a ghoul to heal. There is nothing stopping a vampire from dumping a bunch of vitae into the wound, ghouling them so that they just mystically heal, then letting the ghoul status fall off... ( I have done this twice. )<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Corrigan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164140#post1164140\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Player: &quot;I try some meth.&quot;<br /> ST: &quot;You feel really good. Make your roll. You failed?&quot; &lt;slides Condition card across the table&gt; &quot;Congratulations, you're addicted.&quot;<br /> <br /> I guess I want a little more depth and creep factor than &quot;it tastes really good so you have to do what the scary man says.&quot; That's why I suggested things like funky dreams or other little tweaks that don't involve dice.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like others have pointed out you do not &quot;HAVE TO&quot; do what the scary man says. Also... not all vampires are scary. Not all vampires do it with force. Some do it with pleasure and seduction. One might not even know they have been rolled by a Daeva. You might not even know what the hell you are addicted to other than being around this person and drinking from their drinks or having coppery kisses makes you feel better than anything else in your life ever has. They make the best martini's that you have ever tasted and all you do is want to be around them for another one. Every vampire is different and they are not all creepy, though there is nothing stopping you from making them all like that.<br /> <br /> What I suggest is to do some research on cocaine or heroine or ecstasy, then take the effects and amplify them.<br /> <br /> In my games, I have it a little different for each clan. Drinking Daeva blood is like amped up Molly with the high of the aftermath of great sex. Nos is like cracked out Meth, Gangrel more like aggressive cocaine. Have your players research, the internet is full of great resources if you have not experienced it yourself.<br /> <br /> After the ST slides that card over, it's up to the player to play it out properly. It gives you the tools in the book, if you want your vitae to be like they dropped Acid and LSD by all means, you can have it that way.<br /> <br /> I have changeling blood affect my vampires like LSD does. Hallucinations and all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2308,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","user":"Vitalis","date":". 12-01-2017, 11:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-02T08:26:00-08:00","id":1164537,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164286#post1164286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you want to look at this Mechanically:<br /> <b>&quot;Kindred Vitae</b> is a dangerous food. First off, Vitae is addicting<br /> (see Vitae Addiction, p. 99). As well, it causes the blood bond<br /> (see Blood Bonds, p. 90). However, it\u2019s efficient. Every Vitae<br /> taken is a Vitae earned, and this causes no additional damage<br /> to the victim. Stored Kindred Vitae is <i><u>effectively cold blood</u></i>. &quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Cold blood</b> from a corpse or kept outside the body for<br /> more than a few minutes (but no more than a night) is far<br /> less satisfying. To gain a single point of Vitae, a vampire must<br /> consume as many pints of this blood as her Blood Potency times<br /> two. <u><i>Technologically stored blood, for example in refrigerated<br /> plasma bags, is similarly unpalatable to potent Kindred.</i></u><br /> <br /> Whatever magic is in the blood... dies. As per RAW. It effectively becomes cold blood. Meaning it gains the effective qualities of it. Meaning it is no longer effective. Maybe the fact that the vitae is in a human it stays alive, but once it leaves it's life source it dies.. Even if a human has it one time, maybe that tiny little piece of it stays alive and that's enough to spawn a revenant, but that is not an &quot;Always&quot; event. it is a rare one.</div> </div> </div> </div>I checked back to my group's House Rules file; the blood clarifications section hasn't been touched since we first started playing Requiem, so clearly it doesn't jibe with the current work:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Blood Shelf Life:</b> If kept warm and fluid, blood potency can be maintained for a few hours. Cold-stored blood is about half-strength and unsatisfying. Cold and coagulating in the open air, its half-life shortens to 15 minutes or so. <br /> <br /> <b>Vitae Shelf Life:</b> Short of magical means, the power of vitae dissipates within (BP) minutes, leaving decayed blood of mixed types. While not nourishing, old blood can be used to identify a Kindred for some days. </div> </div> </div>...plus stuff about animal vitae, werewolf blood, etc.<br /> <br /> The updated cold blood-- tying it to BP is interesting. I'll have to get with the others and see what they want to do about that.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164286#post1164286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As per the mechanical rules as they are written. Now, should you want to do that in your game, you are more than welcome to. I change rules and I alter things to. I am sure every one does here and there. I just like to know what the rules are suppose to be and what I am changing them to. Especially if you have a rule monger in your game, you might need to give them a heads up on the matter. Do not take opinion as fact. Do not take story driven fluff as mechanical RAW..</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, that's why I asked. I look at the rules, and decide if RAW 1) makes the most sense to me and 2) fits well within our current setting. Some things I don't like much but not bothered enough to work up an alternative. There is a lot I like about 2nd ed (the changes to Humanity, for instance), and a few things I don't as much but am not bothered enough to alter. A couple points I've changed full stop. By and large, my players are never rules-mongers, so it's more about what would or wouldn't make them stumble if they were reading this chronicle as a story.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164286#post1164286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like others have pointed out you do not &quot;HAVE TO&quot; do what the scary man says.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry, I guess I shouldn't have used that phrase. They don't &quot;have to&quot; do what the pasty-skinned guy wants in the same way the addict doesn't &quot;have to&quot; rob an apartment for drug money or pleasure the dealer when they can't scrounge enough cash to pay for a hit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164286#post1164286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my games, I have it a little different for each clan. Drinking Daeva blood is like amped up Molly with the high of the aftermath of great sex. Nos is like cracked out Meth, Gangrel more like aggressive cocaine.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ooh, I like that. When this is said and done I'll have to compile ideas about Vitae for my notebook.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1163600-vitae-effects-on-mortals?p=1164160#post1164160\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your situation seems to be a player is seeing the effects of it in an elderly couple, so a description of its taste won't really matter but the way it impacts them can.</div> </div> </div> </div>As Storyteller, I need to know what the couple feels in order to play them accurately. Your descriptions help.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2203-corrigan","name":"Corrigan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-02T13:24:34-08:00","id":1164617,"title":"","content":"Why doesn't he want to ghoul them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-02T15:15:59-08:00","id":1164658,"title":"","content":"Aside from the fact that ghouls are an expensive liability and should raise ethical questions about the concept of slavery in anyone claiming a contemporary origin, ghouling an elderly person will attract attention a lot sooner than someone in the 30-50 range. (Just as ghouling a 9 year old would) All of a sudden this elderly couple stops aging AND becomes, stronger, faster or more resilient.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}